    Transaction 1 (Thread A)                          Transaction 2 (Thread B)
          │                                                 │
    SELECT ... FOR UPDATE                                   │
    (Acquires Exclusive Lock in DB)                         │
          │                                                 │
    Business logic execution...                       SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
          │                                  …

**Cascading** in JPA and Hibernate is a declarative mechanism that automatically propagates entity lifecycle state transitions (`PERSIST`, `MERGE`, `REMOVE`, `REFRESH`, `DETACH`) from a parent entity down to its associated child entities. Cascading enables developers to manage domain aggregates through a single entry point (the Aggregate Root), eliminating the need to manually invoke persistence operations on every individual child object.

---

## 🟢 Junior Level

### Core Concept: Why Cascading is Essential

Without cascading, saving a parent `Order` alongside its child `OrderItem` collection requires tedious, manual invocation of `persist()` for every single instance:

```java
// WITHOUT CASCADING (Verbose, error-prone, violates aggregate boundaries):
entityManager.persist(item1);
entityManager.persist(item2);
entityManager.persist(order);
```

With cascading enabled, persistence operations are invoked only on the parent aggregate root:

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    // Operations on Order automatically cascade to all items in the collection:
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
        items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this); // Crucial: Synchronizes the owning side of the relationship
    }
}
```

```java
// WITH CASCADING:
Order order = new Order();
order.addItem(new OrderItem("Laptop"));
order.addItem(new OrderItem("Mouse"));

// Persisting ONLY the parent entity cascades to both child items:
entityManager.persist(order);
// Hibernate automatically generates 3 SQL INSERTs: first for Order, then for each OrderItem
```

### Standard JPA Cascade Types (`CascadeType`)

The JPA specification defines cascade propagation types in the `jakarta.persistence.CascadeType` enum:

| Cascade Type | Propagated `EntityManager` Method | Operational …

The **JPA (Jakarta Persistence)** specification and the **Hibernate** framework provide a set of cascading types that define which entity lifecycle management operations are automatically propagated from a parent entity to its associated child entities. Understanding each cascade type ensures the correct implementation of Domain-Driven Design (DDD) aggregate boundaries and prevents catastrophic accidental deletions of shared data.

---

## 🟢 Junior Level

### 6 Standard JPA `CascadeType` Values

The `jakarta.persistence.CascadeType` enum defines six fundamental cascading strategies:

| Cascade Type | Target `EntityManager` Call | Propagation Behavior |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`PERSIST`** | `entityManager.persist(parent)` | New child entities (`Transient`) are automatically passed to `persist()` and transition to `Managed`. |
| **`MERGE`** | `entityManager.merge(parent)` | Detached child entities (`Detached`) are merged back into the active persistence context. |
| **`REMOVE`** | `entityManager.remove(parent)` | Child records are physically deleted from the database when the parent is deleted. |
| **`REFRESH`** | `entityManager.refresh(parent)` | In-memory child entity state is forcefully refreshed from database rows. |
| **`DETACH`** | `entityManager.detach(parent)` | Associated child entities are evicted from the L1 cache, transitioning to `Detached`. |
| **`ALL`** | All operations simultaneously | Combines all five cascade strategies (`PERSIST`, `MERGE`, `REMOVE`, `REFRESH`, `DETACH`). |

```java
// Example: Combining specific cascade types safely:
@Entity
public class Document {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    // Persist and merge attachments with the document, but disallow cascading deletes:
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "document", cascade = {CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE})
    private List<Attachment> attachments = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

